Keep a vfork() child from rewriting the server's memory

On NOMMU Dropbear vfork()s the session command, and the child runs in the
server's address space until it execs.  Two things it did there broke the
server it resumes:

- signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_DFL).  uClibc's LinuxThreads signal() installs its
  own pthread_sighandler in the kernel and keeps the real handler in a
  table in process memory.  The child's call set the shared table entry
  to SIG_DFL (NULL) while the server's kernel handler stayed
  pthread_sighandler, so the server jumped to address 0 on the SIGCHLD
  that followed the command's exit.  Its SIGSEGV handler then wrote
  "Aiee, segfault!" to stderr, which is the SSH socket under inetd, and the
  client failed with "Bad packet length" instead of getting exit-status.
  execve() resets caught signals to SIG_DFL, so the call is not needed.

- dropbear_exit() when exec (or dup2/chdir) failed.  svr_dropbear_exit()
  skipped session_cleanup() for the child but still freed the host key and
  listen addresses and called exit(), which runs atexit handlers and
  flushes stdio -- all in the server's memory.  Leave with _exit() once
  the message is logged.

--- a/src/dbutil.c
+++ b/src/dbutil.c
@@ -313,8 +313,16 @@
 
 		TRACE(("back to normal sigchld"))
+#if DROPBEAR_VFORK
+		/* Leave SIGCHLD alone: this child still runs in the parent's
+		 * memory, and uClibc's LinuxThreads signal() records the handler
+		 * in a table there, so SIG_DFL would make the parent's next
+		 * SIGCHLD call a NULL handler.  execve() resets caught signals
+		 * to SIG_DFL anyway. */
+#else
 		/* Revert to normal sigchld handling */
 		if (signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_DFL) == SIG_ERR) {
 			dropbear_exit("signal() error");
 		}
+#endif
 
 		/* redirect stdin/stdout */
--- a/src/svr-chansession.c
+++ b/src/svr-chansession.c
@@ -842,8 +842,16 @@
 		
 		TRACE(("back to normal sigchld"))
+#if DROPBEAR_VFORK
+		/* Leave SIGCHLD alone: this child still runs in the parent's
+		 * memory, and uClibc's LinuxThreads signal() records the handler
+		 * in a table there, so SIG_DFL would make the parent's next
+		 * SIGCHLD call a NULL handler.  execve() resets caught signals
+		 * to SIG_DFL anyway. */
+#else
 		/* Revert to normal sigchld handling */
 		if (signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_DFL) == SIG_ERR) {
 			dropbear_exit("signal() error");
 		}
+#endif
 		
 		/* redirect stdin/stdout/stderr */
--- a/src/svr-session.c
+++ b/src/svr-session.c
@@ -281,4 +281,12 @@
 
 #if DROPBEAR_VFORK
+	/* A vfork()ed child (exec failed, or a dup2()/chdir() error) shares
+	 * the server's memory: freeing the host key and addresses below, or
+	 * exit() running atexit handlers and flushing stdio, would corrupt the
+	 * session it returns to.  Leave without touching anything. */
+	if (svr_ses.server_pid != 0 && svr_ses.server_pid != getpid()) {
+		_exit(exitcode);
+	}
+
 	/* For uclinux only the main server process should cleanup - we don't want
 	 * forked children doing that */
